===== HARNESS ADAPTATION: SCHEDULER WAKE UNDER A ONE-SHOT HEADLESS CAPTAIN (2026-09-11T01:58:31Z) =====
node: v24.16.0  bun: 1.3.14
dsh: 0.1.5-rc.1
dsh-subagent: 0.1.5-rc.2

SCOPE
-----
Follow-up to evidence/agent-teams/harness-seam-0.1.5-rc.2/20260911T012022Z-frozen.
That freeze proved the 0.1.5-rc.2 delivery seam (`ctx.subagents.prompt(request, signal)`)
and a green static gate sweep, but its live case (agent-teams-dispatch) still exited 1
with BOTH tasks `pending`/attempt 0 and NO member ever receiving the automatic assignment.
This record resolves that live defect. It is a QA-driver defect plus one QA assertion
defect, NOT a plugin delivery bug: the plugin's scheduler/wake path is unchanged.

SYMPTOM (measured, sandbox /tmp/mpd-dispatch-fdZtqu)
---------------------------------------------------
The captain executed approve -> create_task DISPATCH-PROBE-A (t1) -> create_task
DISPATCH-PROBE-B (t2, depends t1) -> approve (ok, "approved and running (11 members,
2 tasks)"). All 11 members were spawned (subagent/catalog lines 01:30:06.706-07.198).
The captain's single turn then ended at 01:30:10.958 `turn/end {reason: completed}` and
the one-shot process exited. Durable team state (ws/.mpd/team/mpd-default/team.json)
afterwards: phase=running, approvedAt set, t1/t2 both `pending`, attempt 0.
Every member session log held only `turn/start` + `request/header` (15-25 lines), with NO
`turn/end` and NO first assistant message, i.e. every member was disposed during its first
LLM step.

ROOT CAUSE
----------
`packages/mpd-agent-teams-plugin/lib/scheduler.js` is driven by live `agent/status` idle
edges: installTeamScheduler registers
  ctx.on('agent/status', ({ agent, status }) => void syncMemberStatus(...))
and only the `idle` branch calls `runtime.kickMember(...)`. `kickMember` also gates on
`isMemberAvailable(ctx, member)` = (live handle missing) || (live.status === 'idle').
A member that the harness disposes before it ever settles never emits an idle edge in this
process, so the initial `kickTeam` (task mutation) and the idle-edge wake both find nobody
available and dispatch nothing.

`dsh --profile headless "<prompt>"` is ONE-SHOT: the prompt becomes exactly one captain
turn and the process exits the moment that turn ends; process exit disposes every
continuable member. So the headless driver, not the plugin, was starving the scheduler of
the idle edges it keys on. Under the long-lived Web/TUI captain session the plugin is
designed for, members do settle and emit idle edges, so the second batch dispatches (this
record proves it end-to-end below).

QA FIX 1 - hold the captain process open (skills/dsh-qa/scripts/agent-teams-dispatch.mjs)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The probe prompt now has the captain, after approving both tasks, call the bash tool THREE
SEPARATE times with `sleep 110` (not one combined command), then end its turn. The sleeps
only hold the process open; they change no plugin behaviour. Measured on the passing run:
the captain observed "Both tasks completed during the first sleep" and aborted the
remaining sleeps on the third call's return, then ended the turn.

QA FIX 2 - assert the wake on the DEPENDENT assignee, not both members
---------------------------------------------------------------------
The old assertion required the `AgentTeams automatic task assignment` marker (the literal
opening of scheduler.assignmentPrompt) in BOTH member logs and read the session log under
the wrong name. Two measured corrections:
  * log name: this harness writes `session.v3.jsonl.zstd`; the case read only
    `session.jsonl.zstd`, so it scored 0 deliveries on a correct run. Now both names are
    tried (current first, legacy fallback).
  * false negative: on the passing run the first-batch member (Planner, owns
    DISPATCH-PROBE-A, no deps) received only the spawn/join prompt
    ("Current team status: 2 task(s), 1 pending task(s) assigned to you"), self-claimed t1
    with agent_teams_claim_task, and completed it inside its FIRST turn - it is never
    woken again, and that is correct. The member the scheduler must actually wake is the
    dependent one (Researcher, owns DISPATCH-PROBE-B, deps [t1]); it idled after its spawn
    turn and then received the assignment prompt. The case now records each session's
    subagent descriptor label, resolves the dependent task's assignee from live team state,
    and asserts the marker exactly on that assignee's session. Offline self-test grew from
    13 to 14 checks (new check: "real run asserts the wake on the dependent task's assignee
    only").

LIVE PROOF (evidence/dsh-qa/agent-teams-dispatch/2026-09-11T01-52-00.539Z)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
[agent-teams-dispatch] ok=true
  installer:            {"ok":true,"exit":0}
  captainRun:           {"ok":true,"exit":0}
  tasks:                {"a":"completed","b":"completed","total":2}
  memberAssignments:    {"count":1,"sessions":12,"labels":["agent-teams:mpd-default:Researcher"]}
  secondBatchDispatched:{"ok":true}
  assignmentDelivery:   {"ok":true,"dependentAssignee":"Researcher"}
[agent-teams-dispatch] PASS

Member session traces (passing sandbox /tmp/mpd-dispatch-YKFlzp):
  Researcher (owns dependent DISPATCH-PROBE-B): spawn turn idle -> scheduler idle-edge wake
    delivers `AgentTeams automatic task assignment ... Task: t2 - DISPATCH-PROBE-B` -> second
    turn -> agent_teams_update_task status=completed output B-OK.
  Planner (owns DISPATCH-PROBE-A): completed t1 inside its spawn turn from the join prompt
    (no scheduler wake needed), which is why a both-logs marker assertion is a false negative.

COMPARISON (superseded run, 2026-09-11T01-45-58.484Z; pre-edit QA assertion)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  tasks:                {"a":"completed","b":"completed","total":2}
  secondBatchDispatched:{"ok":true}
  assignmentDelivery:   {"ok":false}   <- old both-members marker assertion
The tasks completed and the dependent member WAS woken (Researcher session carried the
marker); only the over-broad assertion failed. This is the run that exposed QA FIX 2.

